One common source of vulnerabilities in Java web applications is the improper handling of user input when performing file operations, which can lead to **Path Traversal** attacks. This occurs when an application uses user-supplied input to construct a file path without sufficient validation.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.file.*;

// VULNERABLE Example: Path Traversal vulnerability
public class FileHandler {
    public void readFile(String userInput) throws IOException {
        // User input is directly concatenated into the file path.
        // This allows an attacker to use "../" to escape the intended directory.
        // For example, passing "../../etc/passwd" would allow reading system files.
        File file = new File("/app/data/" + userInput);

        try (BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(file))) {
            String line;
            while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
                System.out.println(line);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

